# Sales Dashboard

This is the home page of the Sales workbook — the first thing the team sees when
they open `work://sales`. A page in the work format is just a `.work` file with a
single `# Title`, like this one. What makes it a *page* rather than a library unit
is that it is mostly **prose** with a few embedded units that **render by default**:
declared task values, a chart island, an image with a caption. Nothing here is a
mock-up. The board below is the live state of the pipeline; the chart below it
reads the same lead scores the rest of the app computes.

Markdown narrates; it never holds logic. Everything you read in plain sentences —
headings, lists, the caption under the figure — is rich text. Everything in a
`do … end` block (or a `lang :name do … end` island) is a real unit. The two never
mix: prose explains, code runs.

## What this app does

The Sales workbook turns a raw list of company names into a ranked queue of who to
talk to next. The flow is small and honest:

1. **Ingest** a list of leads — name, domain, headcount, revenue.
2. **Enrich** each one against the CRM ([[enrich]] fills the gaps, sets `status: :enriched`).
3. **Score** it ([[score]] turns revenue and size into an integer 0–100).
4. **Rank** the result, and let the analyst agent draft outreach for the top of the list.

The whole sequence is the [[pipeline]] flow; this page is where you watch its
output. The canonical record everything passes around is the `%Lead{}` struct, and
its lifecycle is the enum `@statuses` — `[:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]`.
You will see those same atoms on every page; they are the spine of the app.

## This week's numbers

![Lead scores across the active pipeline, highest first.](work://sales/assets/score-distribution.svg)

*Three companies are in play this week. Initech tops the board at 88 — high
revenue, a real sales team — followed by Acme at 70 and Globex trailing at 41. The
figure above is authored the markdown way: an `![caption](src)` line, then an
optional italic paragraph that becomes the figure's caption. There is no `figure`
keyword in the format — a figure is just an image plus the sentence under it.*

The bar chart further down is the *interactive* version of that same picture: it is
the [[chart]] client island, hydrated in your browser, reading live scores. The
image is the static snapshot; the island is the live one. Same data, two delivery
modes — which is the whole point of the format.

## The board

A page can declare **tasks** inline, and they render as a board. A task is not
markup and not a checkbox — it is a **value of the `Task` struct**, validated at the
weave gate. The fields are `title`, `status` (one of `:todo | :doing | :done`),
`owner` (which must resolve to a real person defined in the workspace), and `ref`
(a deep link to the unit or page the task is about). Because tasks are typed data,
`work check` can tell you a board is wrong — a misspelled owner or a dangling `ref`
fails the build before anyone sees it.

Here is the live state of the sales pipeline work:

data :board, of: Task, from: values([
  %Task{
    title: "Backfill enrichment for Q2 import",
    status: :doing,
    owner: :priya,
    ref: :enrich
  },
  %Task{
    title: "Tune scoring weights — revenue vs. headcount",
    status: :doing,
    owner: :dana,
    ref: :score
  },
  %Task{
    title: "Draft outreach for Initech (score 88)",
    status: :todo,
    owner: :analyst,
    ref: :analyst
  },
  %Task{
    title: "Wire FX rates into deal sizing",
    status: :todo,
    owner: :dana,
    ref: :fx
  },
  %Task{
    title: "Ship the static bundle to the team",
    status: :done,
    owner: :priya,
    ref: :pipeline
  }
])

The board above is **live state**, not a frozen snapshot. Declarative does not mean
immutable: the document *is* the store. When Priya drags the enrichment task to
`:done`, that edits this `data :board` value in place; a draft scopes the change so
two people moving cards at once do not clobber each other. The same `%Task{}` values
power the [[Plan]] page's grouped, swimlane view — this dashboard just shows the
top of the stack.

One subtlety worth naming: `owner: :analyst` does not point at a human. It resolves
to the analyst **agent** — the brain that drafts outreach. Tasks can be owned by
people or by agents interchangeably, because both are addressable units in the
workspace symbol table. When the analyst finishes a draft, it flips its own task to
`:done` the same way Priya does.

## Scores, live

Below is the [[chart]] island — a Svelte client unit that draws a bar per lead. It
runs in the browser as wasm, so it works even in the static bundle with no server
attached. It reads the same scored leads the [[pipeline]] produces; we pass them in
as a typed value rather than letting the island fetch anything itself.

client svelte :chart do
  <script>
    export let leads = [
      { name: "Initech", score: 88, status: "scored" },
      { name: "Acme",    score: 70, status: "scored" },
      { name: "Globex",  score: 41, status: "enriched" }
    ];

    $: ranked = [...leads].sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);
    $: max = Math.max(1, ...ranked.map((l) => l.score));
  </script>

<div class="chart">
  {#each ranked as lead}
    <div class="row">
      <span class="label">{lead.name}</span>
      <div class="track">
        <div
          class="bar"
          class:hot={lead.score >= 80}
          style="width: {(lead.score / max) * 100}%"
        >
          <span class="value">{lead.score}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>

<style>
  .chart { display: grid; gap: 8px; font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui; }
  .row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 80px 1fr; align-items: center; gap: 12px; }
  .label { font-size: 13px; color: #44464d; }
  .track { background: #f1f0ec; border-radius: 6px; overflow: hidden; }
  .bar { background: #aee5c2; padding: 4px 8px; color: #121316; font-weight: 600; border-radius: 6px; }
  .bar.hot { background: #f3c5a3; }
  .value { font-size: 12px; }
</style>
end

The chart re-sorts on every render, so when scoring weights change and Globex jumps
ahead of Acme, the bars reorder with no extra code. For the small inline trend next
to a single lead's detail row we use a different island, the SolidJS [[sparkline]];
this page only needs the full bar comparison.

## How this page ships

The same file you are reading ships three ways, with no rewrite:

- **Static bundle** — `work bundle` weaves the whole `sales/` tree into one gzipped
  `salesapp.html`. The board renders to HTML; the [[chart]] hydrates as an island in
  the browser. No backend required — you can email it.
- **Nexus SSR** — the server regions (anything that touched [[enrich]] or
  [[score]], which are server compute) render on the BEAM; the client islands stay
  islands. The board reflects whatever the live data store currently holds.
- **Live SSR** — the page subscribes over the RCP websocket and the server pushes
  diffs, so a teammate moving a card or the analyst finishing a draft updates this
  board in front of you.

One authoring model, every delivery mode. From here, jump into the [[pipeline]] to
see how leads get scored, open the [[Plan]] for the full work view, or read
[[score]] to understand the number under each bar.
